    tableOfContents: [
      { id: 'what-is-a-high-yield-savings-account', title: 'What Is a High-Yield Savings Account?' },
      { id: 'how-do-they-pay-more', title: 'How Do They Pay More?' },
      { id: 'benefits-of-high-yield-savings', title: 'Benefits of High-Yield Savings' },
      { id: 'what-to-look-for', title: 'What to Look For' },
      { id: 'how-to-maximize-your-earnings', title: 'How to Maximize Your Earnings' },
      { id: 'frequently-asked-questions', title: 'Frequently Asked Questions' },
    ],
    content: [
      { heading: 'What Is a High-Yield Savings Account?', body: [
        'A high-yield savings account is a type of savings account that pays a significantly higher interest rate than a traditional savings account. While a typical savings account at a large traditional bank might pay 0.01% to 0.10% APY, a high-yield savings account can pay many times that rate depending on market conditions.',
        'The difference in earnings can be substantial. On a $10,000 balance, an account paying 0.05% would earn about $5 per year, while an account paying 4.5% would earn about $450 per year. That is money you earn simply for keeping your savings in the right place.',
        'High-yield savings accounts are most commonly offered by online banks and credit unions, though some traditional banks have begun offering competitive rates as well. They function like any other savings account: you deposit money, earn interest, and can withdraw funds when needed.',
      ]},
      { heading: 'How Do They Pay More?', body: [
        'The reason online banks can offer higher rates comes down to operating costs. Traditional banks maintain physical branches, employ large staffs, and pay for real estate and utilities. Online banks operate primarily through websites and mobile apps, which dramatically reduces their overhead.',
        'These savings are passed on to customers in the form of higher interest rates on deposits and lower fees. Without the expense of maintaining hundreds of branch locations, online banks can afford to pay more for the money you deposit.',
        'It is worth understanding that interest rates on high-yield savings accounts are variable, meaning they can change at any time. They are influenced by broader economic factors, particularly the federal funds rate set by the central bank. When that rate rises, savings rates typically follow.',
      ]},
      { heading: 'Benefits of High-Yield Savings', body: [
        'The primary benefit is earning more on your savings, but there are other advantages. High-yield savings accounts keep your money accessible. Unlike certificates of deposit, which lock your money in for a set term, you can withdraw from a savings account when you need it.',
        'Your money is protected. High-yield accounts at FDIC-insured banks are covered up to $250,000 per depositor, per institution, per ownership category. At NCUA-insured credit unions, the same coverage applies. This means your savings are safe even if the bank fails.',
        'There is no investment risk. Unlike stocks or bonds, the value of your savings does not fluctuate with the market. Your principal is protected, and you earn a predictable return. This makes high-yield savings ideal for money you cannot afford to lose, like your emergency fund.',
        'Many high-yield accounts have no minimum balance requirements and no monthly fees, making them accessible to savers at any level.',
      ]},
      { heading: 'What to Look For', body: [
        'When choosing a high-yield savings account, the APY is the most obvious factor, but do not chase rates blindly. Banks sometimes offer promotional rates that drop after a few months. Look for accounts with consistently competitive rates rather than temporary teasers.',
        'Check for fees and minimums. The best accounts have no monthly maintenance fees and no minimum balance requirements, but some accounts do have these, so read the fine print. Consider the withdrawal options and how long transfers take to external accounts.',
        'Evaluate the mobile app and website. Since you will be managing your account entirely online, a good digital experience matters. Look for banks with well-reviewed apps that make it easy to check balances, transfer money, and set up automatic savings.',
      ]},
      { heading: 'How to Maximize Your Earnings', body: [
        'To get the most from a high-yield savings account, keep your emergency fund there. An emergency fund of three to six months of expenses can earn meaningful interest in a high-yield account, whereas it would earn almost nothing in a traditional savings account.',
        'Set up automatic transfers. Many banks let you schedule recurring transfers from checking to savings. Automating your savings ensures you consistently build your balance without having to think about it.',
        'Compare rates periodically. Because rates are variable and banks adjust them at different times, the account with the best rate today may not have the best rate in six months. It is reasonable to check rates once or twice a year, but avoid rate-chasing that causes you to constantly open and close accounts.',
      ]},
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: 'Are high-yield savings account rates guaranteed?', answer: 'No, the rates are variable and can change at any time. They generally move with broader interest rate trends. Your principal is protected, but the rate you earn is not locked in.' },
      { question: 'How much should I keep in a high-yield savings account?', answer: 'Most financial experts recommend keeping three to six months of living expenses in savings as an emergency fund. Any money you might need in the short term is also well-suited for a high-yield savings account.' },
      { question: 'Can I lose money in a high-yield savings account?', answer: 'Your principal is protected by FDIC insurance up to $250,000 per institution. You will not lose your deposited money. However, if the interest rate is lower than inflation, your money loses purchasing power over time.' },
      { question: 'Do I have to pay taxes on savings account interest?', answer: 'Yes, interest earned on savings accounts is taxable as ordinary income. You will receive a 1099-INT form from your bank if you earn more than $10 in interest during the year.' },
    ],
